Until this point, the investigation had progressed primarily through conversations between ChatGPT and me. We examined possible explanations, questioned their assumptions, and considered how they related to my experience as a musician.

As the investigation continued, I began to wonder whether my involvement was limiting its progress. I asked ChatGPT whether I was becoming a bottleneck in the investigation.

ChatGPT suggested that the limitation lay less in my involvement than in the way the investigation was structured. Each subsequent step depended largely on our preceding discussion. Although this allowed me to examine the reasoning as it developed, not every part of the investigation required my participation.

I proposed separating the investigation into two paths.

The first would continue through conversations between ChatGPT and me. We would examine musical experiences, discuss possible explanations, and question the assumptions behind them.

The second would be conducted autonomously by AI. It would examine existing theoretical and empirical accounts, formulate hypotheses, and explore possible counterexamples without requiring my involvement at every step.

I also wanted the second path to begin without detailed knowledge of the observations we had made in the first. Rather than developing explanations around those observations, it could produce explanations against which the observations might later be examined.

The two paths would share the same research question, but neither would be required to adopt the explanations developed by the other.

I want to allow the investigation to proceed beyond the questions I happen to ask.
